content.thinking:
The persistence scenario fails. The issue is in the "page is reloaded" step. When we reload, we create a new happy-dom window, which clears localStorage. But we're trying to restore it. The problem is that `this.loadApp()` creates a fresh window, and the localStorage from the old window doesn't carry over.

Let me look at the step definition for "the page is reloaded":

```js
When('the page is reloaded', function () {
  // Capture current localStorage state
  const stored = this.window.localStorage.getItem('todos-vanillajs');
  // Reload the app
  this.loadApp();
  // Restore localStorage (loadApp creates fresh window)
  if (stored) {
    this.window.localStorage.setItem('todos-vanillajs', stored);
  }
  // Re-trigger routing
  this.window.dispatchEvent(new this.window.Event('load'));
});
```

The problem is that `this.loadApp()` creates a completely new Window instance, which has its own isolated localStorage. When I retrieve the stored data from the old window and then create a new one, the localStorage is empty again. I need to set the item on the new window's localStorage before the app initializes, so it picks up the persisted data during its load sequence.

Setting localStorage after `loadApp()` won't work because the app has already run its initialization logic and won't re-read from storage. The real issue is that happy-dom creates a fresh in-memory localStorage for each new Window instance, so I need to either set the data before the app loads or trigger a reload mechanism that the app itself handles. I'm realizing the core issue: `loadApp()` creates a fresh window with empty localStorage, and even though I set the stored data afterward, the app has already run and won't re-read it. I need to either preload the localStorage before calling `loadApp()`, or find a way to make the app reinitialize after the data is restored. I see the core issue now—the app runs its initialization when `loadApp()` is called, so setting localStorage afterward doesn't help since the app has already loaded with empty storage. The real problem is that I need to populate localStorage before the app initializes, but the current setup creates a fresh window each time. I'm considering whether I can manually set up the DOM and app state without going through the full `loadApp()` cycle, or if there's a way to intercept the initialization.

